  "enhanced_information": "The patient, a 57-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis on corticosteroids, presents with epigastric pain and a 1 cm ulcer with signs of acute inflammation and intestinal metaplasia. Corticosteroid use is known to increase the risk of peptic ulcers. The presence of intestinal metaplasia raises concern for potential malignancy, necessitating careful management. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) are the standard treatment for peptic ulcers, promoting healing and reducing gastric acid secretion. Follow-up with a control gastroscopy and biopsy at 8 weeks is essential to monitor for healing and any signs of malignancy. While Helicobacter pylori eradication is important in cases of infection, the biopsy results suggest a more complex pathology that warrants initial medical therapy with PPIs. Surgical intervention is not first-line for ulcer management unless there are complications. Therefore, the most appropriate treatment option is treatment with proton pump inhibitors and a control gastroscopy with biopsy at 8 weeks."
